How the script works (short)
- Heuristics used to mark processes as suspicious:
  - Exact suspicious names like sysd, kworkerplus, kworkerd (examples you gave).
  - Executable marked “(deleted)” in /proc or executable path located in writable/temp directories (/tmp,
/var/tmp, /dev/shm, /run).
  - Command lines containing known miner strings (xmrig, minerd, stratum, --pool, --threads, etc.).
- For each matched PID the script logs process info, attempts a graceful kill, then sends SIGKILL if 
needed.
- The script writes a human-readable log at /workspace/terminate_miner.log.

How to use it later
- Run as root (or with sudo) so it can terminate other users' processes:
  sudo /workspace/terminate_miner.sh
- If you see CPU spikes, run it while the spike occurs so it can catch transient processes.
- Inspect the log after running:
  cat /workspace/terminate_miner.log

Notes and safety
- The script only kills processes that match the stronger heuristics above to reduce false positives (it 
does not blindly kill arbitrary system processes).
- A transient /proc/... read error can appear if a process vanishes while scanning; that’s normal and 
harmless.

If you want, I can:
- Make the heuristics more aggressive or more conservative (tell me which).
- Add automatic periodic checks (cron/systemd timer) or a one-liner to monitor CPU spikes and invoke the 
script.
- Search for persistence (crontabs, user/systemd services, startup scripts) and remove suspicious startup
entries.

