Final report — actions taken and current status

What I did (automated and immediate)
- Created a safe termination script: /workspace/terminate_miner.sh
  - Heuristics: suspicious process names (e.g. sysd, kworkerplus), deleted executables, executables 
running from writable temp dirs (/tmp, /var/tmp, /dev/shm, /run), and command-line miner indicators 
(xmrig, minerd, stratum, --pool, --threads, etc.).
  - Behavior: logs info to /workspace/terminate_miner.log, attempts graceful kill then SIGKILL.
  - Usage: sudo /workspace/terminate_miner.sh

- Created a continuous monitor: /workspace/monitor_and_terminate.sh
  - Watches for processes exceeding THRESHOLD (default 20% CPU) every SLEEP seconds (default 5s).
  - When a high-CPU process is seen it applies the same heuristics and invokes terminate_miner.sh if 
suspicious.
  - Monitor log: /workspace/terminate_miner_monitor.log
  - nohup output: /workspace/monitor_and_terminate.out
  - PID recorded: /workspace/monitor_and_terminate.pid (monitor started in background)

- Ran scans and checks for persistence and active miners:
  - Listed top CPU processes (ps aux) and searched for suspicious names (sysd, kworkerplus, etc.).
  - Grepped common locations for miner indicators: /etc, /home, /var/spool/cron, /etc/systemd, 
/lib/systemd, /opt, /tmp, /var/tmp, /dev/shm.
  - Inspected /etc/crontab and per-user crontabs and /etc/init.d and rc.local.
  - Searched for executable files in writable temp directories and for large executables in common 
locations.
  - Result: No active miner processes or obvious persistence artifacts were found during these 
inspections.

Current status
- No active miner process was detected at the time of inspection.
- The monitor is running and will attempt to catch and terminate transient high-CPU suspicious processes 
automatically. Its PID is in /workspace/monitor_and_terminate.pid.

Where to look for evidence if spikes occur
- If you observe a CPU spike, either manually run the terminator or let the monitor capture it. Useful 
logs:
  - /workspace/terminate_miner.log
  - /workspace/terminate_miner_monitor.log
  - /workspace/monitor_and_terminate.out

Quick commands
- Run termination manually (recommended during spike):
  sudo /workspace/terminate_miner.sh

- Stop the monitor:
  kill $(cat /workspace/monitor_and_terminate.pid) || kill <PID>

- Tail logs:
  tail -n 200 /workspace/terminate_miner_monitor.log
  tail -n 200 /workspace/terminate_miner.log

Notes and recommendations
- The scripts use conservative heuristics to avoid killing legitimate system processes. If you want a 
more aggressive policy (e.g., kill any unknown process above X% CPU), I can update the scripts to be more
aggressive and add an explicit whitelist/blacklist.
- To capture network behavior during a spike I can add an ss/lsof capture to the monitor (records remote 
IPs/ports used by suspicious processes).
- For persistent automatic startup, I can create a systemd unit to launch the monitor at boot (requires 
root and a running systemd).

Conclusion
- I implemented tools to detect and terminate likely crypto-miners, started a background monitor to catch
transient spikes, and thoroughly scanned common persistence locations. No active miner was found during 
the inspection, but the monitor will act automatically on future spikes.

